---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ProjectHeader from '../../components/ProjectHeader.astro';
import Chart from '../../components/Chart.astro';
import NeuroPipeline from '../../components/NeuroPipeline.astro';

// Aggregate, cohort-level results only. No patient-level data is shown on this page.
// Error = patient-level relative error, mean ± SD across held-out patients (patient-split CV).
// Zero-neurogenesis patients are excluded everywhere except the zero-patient section.

// Encoder comparison (NeuroGNN embedding + phase-coherence matrix unless noted)
const encoders = [
  { label: 'Detection NeuroGNN (previous)', value: 70.58, sd: 48.81, pls: 6, slot: 'ref' },
  { label: 'Seizure-type NeuroGNN', value: 68.9, sd: 31.61, pls: 9, slot: 1 },
  { label: 'Phase coherence only', value: 69.21, sd: 38.45, pls: 4, slot: 2 },
  { label: 'Self-supervised NeuroGNN', value: 76.88, sd: 38.16, pls: 1, slot: 'ref' },
].map((e) => ({ ...e, note: `± ${e.sd.toFixed(2)} SD · ${e.pls} PLS components` }));

// Leave-one-input-out, retrained and re-tuned per case. Δ vs baseline 70.58 ± 48.81 (pp).
// Positive Δ error = removing the input hurts (the input helps).
const singles: [string, number, number, number][] = [
  // [input, Δ error, Δ SD, best PLS]
  ['Fp1', 0.97, 1.37, 6], ['Fp2', -1.89, -2.97, 6], ['F3', -0.33, -4.7, 8], ['F4', -0.99, -6.93, 8],
  ['C3', 1.23, -0.65, 6], ['C4', -0.55, -7.12, 8], ['P3', -0.3, -1.06, 6], ['P4', -1.71, -1.9, 6],
  ['O1', -1.19, -3.61, 6], ['O2', 0.34, -3.63, 8], ['F7', 0.01, -0.84, 6], ['F8', 0.62, -6.36, 6],
  ['T3', 1.69, -4.44, 6], ['T4', 3.5, -4.35, 7], ['T5', -6.35, -24.43, 5], ['T6', 0.18, -3.9, 8],
  ['Fz', -5.4, -8.44, 8], ['Cz', 2.45, -7.11, 7], ['Pz', -6.22, -12.61, 6],
  ['Age', -0.21, 0.16, 6], ['Disease duration', 0.86, -0.37, 6], ['Laterality', -1.96, -3.54, 6],
  ['Seizure frequency', -0.53, -0.13, 6], ['Seizure length', -0.78, -1.19, 6], ['Phase coherence', 0.58, -9.59, 9],
];
const singleItems = [...singles]
  .sort((a, b) => b[1] - a[1])
  .map(([label, value, sd, pls]) => ({
    label, value,
    slot: label === 'Disease duration' ? 1 : 'ref',
    note: `Δ SD ${sd > 0 ? '+' : ''}${sd.toFixed(2)} pp · ${pls} PLS components`,
  }));

const groups: [string, number, number, number][] = [
  ['Same-side + midline', 26.34, 31.26, 20],
  ['Same-side', 9.64, -14.25, 3],
  ['Different-side', 0.31, -26.53, 4],
  ['Different-side + midline', -1.17, -28.4, 4],
  ['Midline', -3.23, -10.41, 6],
];
const groupItems = groups.map(([label, value, sd, pls]) => ({
  label, value,
  slot: label === 'Same-side + midline' ? 1 : 'ref',
  note: `Δ SD ${sd > 0 ? '+' : ''}${sd.toFixed(2)} pp · ${pls} PLS components`,
}));

// Clip type, with the number of clips per patient held fixed (patients with ≥ 30 seizure clips only)
const clipMix = [
  { label: '30 seizure clips', value: 72.23, sd: 58.84, pls: 5, slot: 'ref' },
  { label: '30 non-seizure clips', value: 67.98, sd: 29.64, pls: 2, slot: 'ref' },
  { label: '15 seizure + 15 non-seizure', value: 57.92, sd: 31.74, pls: 5, slot: 1 },
].map((e) => ({ ...e, note: `± ${e.sd.toFixed(2)} SD · ${e.pls} PLS components` }));

// Zero-neurogenesis patients included
const zeros = [
  { label: 'Gate + head trained with zeros', value: 300.21, sd: 458.38, pls: 6, slot: 1 },
  { label: 'No gate, head trained with zeros', value: 306.62, sd: 454.68, pls: 6, slot: 2 },
  { label: 'Gate, head trained without zeros', value: 5124.63, sd: 8896.37, pls: 3, slot: 'ref' },
  { label: 'No gate, head trained without zeros', value: 8720.18, sd: 7770.8, pls: 3, slot: 'ref' },
].map((e) => ({ ...e, note: `± ${e.sd.toLocaleString('en-US')} SD · ${e.pls} PLS components` }));

const sdRow = (e: { label: string; value: number; sd: number; pls: number }) =>
  [e.label, `${e.value.toFixed(2)} ± ${e.sd.toFixed(2)}`, e.pls];
const dRow = ([k, v, sd, pls]: [string, number, number, number]) =>
  [k, (v > 0 ? '+' : '') + v.toFixed(2), (sd > 0 ? '+' : '') + sd.toFixed(2), pls];
---
<Base title="NeurogenesisGNN" description="Estimating patient-level hippocampal neurogenesis from EEG and clinical variables when thousands of clips come from only a few dozen patients.">
  <ProjectHeader
    title="NeurogenesisGNN"
    subtitle="Estimating hippocampal neurogenesis from EEG when thousands of clips come from only 30 patients."
    status="Ongoing"
    role="Part-time research assistant · owner of the code base and ML pipeline"
    period="Jun 2026 – present"
    team="Collaboration between the Bonaguidi Lab (USC) and USC InfoLab / IMSC"
    tags={['Small-n / high-d learning', 'Evaluation design', 'Biomedical ML', 'Interpretability']}
    tldr={[
      '<strong>13,000 clips ≠ 13,000 samples.</strong> The effective sample size is about 30 patients, so the prediction head is a statistical model (PLS + Ridge) on frozen EEG embeddings, not a deep network.',
      '<strong>An early finding did not survive a stricter test.</strong> With clips split at random, disease duration looked like one of the most useful inputs. With patients split and the model retrained for every ablation, removing it barely matters; the model was probably recognising patients through it.',
      'Under patient-level evaluation, <strong>mixing seizure and non-seizure clips</strong> lowers error from ~68–72% to ~58%, and the channels on the <strong>side where tissue was sampled</strong> carry the most signal. Zero-neurogenesis patients remain the open problem.',
    ]}
  />

  <h2>The question</h2>
  <p>
    Hippocampal neurogenesis is reported to decline in epilepsy, but it can only be measured in brain tissue. Our collaborators in the
    Bonaguidi Lab asked whether it could be <em>estimated</em> from scalp EEG plus clinical variables such as age and disease duration.
    Alisha Cayce (Bonaguidi Lab) proposed using <strong>NeuroGNN</strong>, an EEG graph model developed at USC InfoLab (not my work),
    because it combines temporal, spatial and semantic structure. My job was to turn that idea into a working
    system: adapt NeuroGNN as a feature extractor and build the data pipeline, prediction head, evaluation and analysis around it.
  </p>

  <h2>The data regime decides the model</h2>
  <div class="stats">
    <div class="stat"><div class="num">30</div><div class="lbl">patients: the real number of independent samples</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="num">≈13k</div><div class="lbl">60-second EEG clips (correlated within patient)</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="num">≈6.4k</div><div class="lbl">embedding dimensions per clip</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="num">9</div><div class="lbl">patients with zero measured neurogenesis</div></div>
  </div>
  <p>
    I noticed the mismatch on day one. Clips from the same patient are not independent, so ~13k clips do not give a deep prediction head
    enough signal, and a ~6.4k-dimensional input with this few subjects overfits easily. I compared Lasso, Ridge, SVR, PLS and
    <strong>PLS + Ridge</strong>, and the combination worked best. PLS finds a few directions in the embedding that covary with the label
    (supervised dimensionality reduction). Ridge then shrinks the weights on those components. Every step, scaling included, is fitted on
    training folds only.
  </p>
  <p>
    Turning a patient’s clip predictions into one number also matters. The clip predictions have heavy outliers, so I first used the
    <strong>median</strong>. Looking at individual patients showed the median was often not the closest percentile. The
    <strong>calibrator</strong> learns from training patients which percentile of the clip-prediction distribution usually lands nearest
    the true label, then reads that percentile off each test patient’s distribution.
  </p>

  <NeuroPipeline />

  <h2>A result that did not survive</h2>
  <p>
    Early on, patient-level folds were too unstable to guide development (some folds held a single patient with a few minutes of EEG),
    so I used clip-level cross-validation as a development signal. Its first input ablation looked clinically sensible:
    <strong>disease duration</strong> came out among the most useful inputs, in line with reports that neurogenesis declines the longer
    epilepsy lasts. Two things about that experiment were wrong:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Leakage through the patient.</strong> With clips split at random, the same patient appears in training and test. Disease
      duration is constant within a patient, so it can work as a patient ID: the model sees a familiar value and returns a familiar label.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Removing an input is not the same as not having it.</strong> I removed each input from a model already trained on all of them.
      PLS + Ridge weights features by how they relate to the training labels, so that model may lean on a few inputs and barely use the rest.
      Dropping an input it never relied on shows nothing about whether the input carries information.
    </li>
  </ul>
  <p>
    So I changed both. Cross-validation now splits <strong>by patient</strong>, so test patients are never seen in training, and every input
    combination is <strong>retrained and re-tuned from scratch</strong>, including the number of PLS components. To keep runs fast and
    comparable, these controlled experiments use a subset of the data: 30 non-seizure clips and up to 30 seizure clips per patient.
  </p>
  <div class="stats">
    <div class="stat"><div class="num">+12.3 pp</div><div class="lbl">error increase from removing disease duration: clip-level split, removed at evaluation</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="num">+0.9 pp</div><div class="lbl">the same input under patient-level split, model retrained without it</div></div>
    <div class="stat"><div class="num">29% → 71%</div><div class="lbl">baseline error, clip-level split vs patient-level split (controlled subset): the honest number is much worse</div></div>
  </div>
  <p>
    My reading is that the earlier model was partly memorising patients through disease duration. That is a hypothesis, but the size of the
    change, and the jump in baseline error once patients are held out, both point the same way. The old ablation is retired.
  </p>

  <h2>Which encoder?</h2>
  <p>
    With the stricter setup in place, I compared EEG encoders. Each one is combined with the phase-coherence matrix (a simple measure of how
    synchronised each pair of channels is), except the last configuration, which uses the coherence matrix alone.
  </p>
  <Chart
    title="Patient-level relative error by EEG encoder (lower is better)"
    spec={{ type: 'hbar', items: encoders, unit: '%', decimals: 1, xLabel: 'mean patient-level relative error (%)' }}
    caption="The NeuroGNN trained for seizure-type classification is slightly better than the detection model used so far, and clearly more stable across patients (SD 31.6 vs 48.8). The phase-coherence matrix alone almost matches it. With only ~21 non-zero patients and standard deviations of 30–50 points, gaps of 1–2 points are not meaningful on their own; the coherence result is the more interesting one."
    table={{ head: ['Encoder / inputs', 'Error (%) mean ± SD', 'PLS comp.'], rows: encoders.map(sdRow) }}
  />

  <h2>What the inputs contribute, measured properly</h2>
  <p>
    The ablation, rerun with patient splits and retraining. Baseline: 70.58% ± 48.81. Values are changes in percentage points; positive means
    removing the input <em>hurts</em>. How I read the two directions: if removing an input changes nothing, it either carries little
    information or other inputs already cover it. If removing it <em>helps</em>, it may add noise, or let the model learn patterns that work
    for some training patients but do not transfer to new ones.
  </p>
  <Chart
    title="Change in patient-level error when one input is removed (pp)"
    spec={{ type: 'hbar', items: singleItems, signed: true, decimals: 1, xLabel: 'Δ error when removed (pp) · positive = the input helps' }}
    caption="Disease duration (highlighted) now sits next to zero. Removing T5, Pz or Fz lowers error by 5–6 points; T5 also cuts the spread across patients by 24 points. Single-channel effects of a few points are within the noise of this cohort and are leads to check, not conclusions."
    table={{ head: ['Removed input', 'Δ error (pp)', 'Δ SD (pp)', 'PLS comp.'], rows: singles.map(dRow) }}
  />
  <p>
    Grouping channels by the hemisphere where the patient’s brain tissue was sampled tells a clearer story than single channels:
  </p>
  <Chart
    title="Removing channel groups: change in patient-level error (pp)"
    spec={{ type: 'hbar', items: groupItems, signed: true, decimals: 1, xLabel: 'Δ error when removed (pp) · positive = the channels help' }}
    caption="“Same-side” = channels over the hemisphere where tissue was sampled; “midline” = Fz, Cz, Pz."
    table={{ head: ['Removed channels', 'Δ error (pp)', 'Δ SD (pp)', 'PLS comp.'], rows: groups.map(dRow) }}
  />
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Same-side channels matter most.</strong> Removing them costs 9.6 points; removing them together with the midline costs 26.3 points and nearly doubles the spread across patients. This fits the measurement being taken on that side, but it is a correlation in this cohort, not a mechanism.</li>
    <li><strong>Different-side channels mostly add variance.</strong> Without them the mean error is unchanged, but the standard deviation drops by 26.5 points, more than half.</li>
    <li><strong>The midline interacts.</strong> Removing it alone <em>helps</em> by 3.2 points, yet removing it together with the same-side channels hurts far more than removing the same-side channels alone. I do not have an explanation for this yet.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Seizure clips, non-seizure clips, or both?</h2>
  <p>
    Here I kept the number of clips per patient fixed and changed only their type. Only patients with at least 30 seizure clips are included,
    so these numbers are not directly comparable with the baseline above.
  </p>
  <Chart
    title="Patient-level relative error by clip type (30 clips per patient)"
    spec={{ type: 'hbar', items: clipMix, unit: '%', decimals: 1, xLabel: 'mean patient-level relative error (%)' }}
    caption="With the clip count held equal, the mix beats both seizure-only and non-seizure-only training by 10–14 points. The two clip types seem to carry complementary information."
    table={{ head: ['Clips per patient', 'Error (%) mean ± SD', 'PLS comp.'], rows: clipMix.map(sdRow) }}
  />

  <h2>A second regime: zero neurogenesis</h2>
  <p>
    Plotting prediction quality patient by patient showed that most severe outliers were patients with <em>zero</em> measured neurogenesis,
    which is why they are excluded above. There are two possible reasons, and I cannot yet tell them apart. One is measurement: the density
    of new neurons in resected tissue drops quickly, so a sample that is not processed right away can read as zero, and some zeros may be
    handling artefacts. The other is biology: the zero group may be a genuinely different, non-linear regime that a linear head such as
    PLS + Ridge cannot express. On top of both, relative error is close to undefined near zero, since a small miss becomes a huge percentage.
  </p>
  <p>
    I tested a logistic zero/non-zero gate (hurdle model) in front of the regressor, and whether the regression head sees zero patients
    during training:
  </p>
  <Chart
    title="Patient-level relative error with zero-neurogenesis patients included (log scale)"
    spec={{ type: 'hbar', items: zeros, log: true, unit: '%', decimals: 0, xLabel: 'relative error (%, log scale)' }}
    caption="What matters is whether the head has seen zeros: errors fall from thousands of percent to ~300%. Under patient-level evaluation the gate itself adds almost nothing (300% vs 307%). Even the best setup is far from usable, which supports the concern that the current features do not contain enough information to recognise zero-neurogenesis patients."
    table={{ head: ['Configuration', 'Error (%) mean ± SD', 'PLS comp.'], rows: zeros.map(sdRow) }}
  />

  <h2>What this project taught me</h2>
  <p>
    How to work with data that are scarce, heterogeneous and unevenly distributed: choosing models by effective sample size, being explicit
    about what each evaluation can show, and finding subgroups that break a model. Also that a result can be plausible, consistent with the
    literature, and still be leakage; the evaluation has to match the claim before the result means anything. It was also my first time
    adapting someone else’s model as a component for a new objective.
  </p>

  <h2>Next</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Repeat the controlled experiments on the full dataset, with patient-level cross-validation throughout.</li>
    <li>Follow up the channel findings: the same-side/midline interaction, and why removing T5, Fz or Pz helps.</li>
    <li>Separate the two explanations for zero-neurogenesis patients, and model that regime separately if it is real.</li>
    <li>Per-patient prediction variance as a first-class output, a prerequisite for anything clinical.</li>
    <li>Add MRI as a further modality; explore fine-tuning the encoder on this task.</li>
  </ul>
</Base>
